<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palace of the Grand Master of the Knights of Rhodes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palace of the Grand Master is a medieval fortress-palace in the old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> in the 14th century on the site of an earlier citadel. Badly damaged by an explosion in 1856, it was rebuilt by the Italian authorities in the 1930s. It is part of the Medieval City of Rhodes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The palace, also known as the Kastello, is one of the few examples of Gothic architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Recent study suggests that the foundations of the ancient temple of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a> lay on the same spot, probably where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes">Colossus of Rhodes</a> stood in antiquity. A Byzantine citadel was built there in the late 7th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1309, they made the fortress their administrative centre and the palace of their Grand Master, repairing it and making major changes in the first quarter of the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The palace was damaged in the earthquake of 1481 and repaired soon afterwards. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> captured the island in 1522 it served as a command centre and fortress. In 1856 lightning struck a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> magazine under the nearby Church of Saint John, possibly stored there since the siege of 1522. The explosion killed many people, destroyed the church and brought down most of the palace's upper floors, though the ground-floor rooms survived.</p><p>Under Italian rule the Ottoman-era structures were demolished, and the architect Vittorio Mesturino was asked to design the reconstruction of the palace and of the nearby Street of the Knights. With few remains or records of the earlier building, he created a structure that also met the authorities' need for a working government building drawing on the Hospitaller legacy. The works took place between 1937 and 1940. Critics have faulted its lack of historical accuracy and its changes to the authentic remains; one scholar called it &quot;horrendous fascist taste&quot;. It became a holiday residence for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> and later for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, whose name remains on a plaque near the entrance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Treaty of Peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> of 10 February 1947 transferred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese Islands</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the handover took place in 1948. The palace was then turned into a museum. In 1988, while Greece held the rotating presidency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a> and the other leaders of the community met there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palace of the Grand Master of the Knights of Rhodes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The order ran a hospital, built great walls and the <b>Palace of the Grand Master</b>, and used its galleys against Muslim shipping.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-rhodes">Hospitaller Rhodes</a></li><li><blockquote>Mario Lago (1922-1936) invested in roads, agriculture, archaeology and buildings, including the restored <b>Palace of the Grand Master</b> and new Italian rationalist architecture.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-islands-of-the-aegean">Italian Islands of the Aegean</a></li></ul></section>
